bf16 primitive inventory + real matmul numbers on Phoenix (XDNA1) -- pivot away
from the groupnorm_bf16 splice thread toward a compute-bound op

Context: the groupnorm_bf16 kernel (results/aie/groupnorm_bf16_handoff_floor_npu.log)
is memory-bound -- ~6 ops per element, no arithmetic intensity to speak of -- so bf16
there only ever bought a 2x byte-size reduction over fp32, never a compute win, and
every fix to the two-process handoff floor was fighting a boundary cost, not the real
constraint. Decision: stop optimizing a memory-bound splice and instead check whether
a compute-bound op (fused self-attention: QK^T -> softmax -> PV) is buildable entirely
from mlir-aie's own validated bf16 building blocks on this exact chip, so bf16's actual
advantage on AIE2 (native bf16xbf16->fp32 MAC) has something to land on.

--- 1. bf16 primitive inventory (static code reading, v1.4.2, C:\Users\<user>\mlir-aie)
---

| Primitive | bf16 on Phoenix (npu1/aie2)? | Path |
|---|---|---|
| matmul (single_core, whole_array) | Yes, in-tree bf16 lit tests | basic/matrix_multiplication/{single_core,whole_array} |
| eltwise (add/mul) | Yes | ml/eltwise |
| eltwise_unary (relu/silu/gelu) | Yes | ml/eltwise_unary |
| scale_shift | Yes | ml/scale_shift |
| softmax | Yes | ml/softmax |
| swiglu | Yes | ml/swiglu |
| conv2d / conv2d_14x14 / bottleneck / resnet | No -- int8/uint8 only, no bf16 path anywhere | ml/conv2d*, ml/bottleneck, ml/resnet |
| LayerNorm / RMSNorm | bf16, but Strix (aie2p) only -- "no aie2 counterpart" | ml/norm |
| RoPE | bf16, Strix (aie2p) only | ml/rope |
| dwconv1d | bf16, Strix (aie2p) only | ml/dwconv1d |
| cast_f32_bf16 | Strix (aie2p) only | ml/cast_f32_bf16 |

Conclusion: a CNN path has no bf16 conv2d to build on (would mean writing one from
zero, no in-tree reference). A self-attention block (matmul + softmax + matmul, no
LayerNorm) is assemblable entirely from primitives already validated in-tree for this
chip. This picked the candidate net -- see docs/DECISIONS.md and RESEARCH.md for the
full reasoning chain.

`ml/resnet/layers_conv2_x` (already run, mlir_aie_magika_mobilenet_npu.log) is the
structural reference for chaining blocks without a host round-trip: block N's output
ObjectFifo is literally block N+1's input ObjectFifo (core-to-core, no L3 hop), MemTile
is used only for buffering/broadcast (skip connections, weight staging), and the whole
chain is one Runtime.sequence / one xclbin / one dispatch.

--- 2. Real hardware bf16 matmul numbers (this session, this machine) ---

Before this session, no design in `basic/matrix_multiplication` had been run on this
machine at all -- only `getting_started/03_matrix_multiplication_single_core` (int16)
had been tried previously. Running these bf16-tagged designs for the first time hit
five new native-Windows toolchain issues, all fixed at invocation time (see section 3);
none required editing the design's actual kernel/MLIR.

single_core, 512x512x512, bf16 in/out, run via the pure-Python IRON path
(`python3 single_core.py --dev npu -M 512 -K 512 -N 512 --dtype_in bf16 --dtype_out bf16
--warmup 1 --iters 5`):

  NPU time     (avg/min/max us): 2303.0 / 2289.5 / 2318.6
  End-to-end   (avg/min/max us): 3043.1 / 2921.6 / 3247.4
  NPU GFLOPS                    : 116.56
  PASS!

whole_array, 4 columns, 512x512x512, bf16 in/out, same path
(`python3 whole_array.py --dev npu -M 512 -K 512 -N 512 --n-aie-cols 4 --dtype_in bf16
--dtype_out bf16 --warmup 1 --iters 5`):

  NPU time     (avg/min/max us): 299.9 / 293.1 / 308.0
  End-to-end   (avg/min/max us): 848.5 / 740.3 / 1224.2
  NPU GFLOPS                    : 895.08
  PASS!

4 columns scale single-core throughput by ~7.7x (116.56 -> 895.08 GFLOPS), not a clean
4x -- consistent with whole_array using more compute tiles per column than single_core
uses in total, not just more columns.

**bf16 matmul is real and correct on this hardware, verified end to end (PASS, not just
timed)** -- this is the headline finding, not the GFLOPS number. It answers the
blocking question the pivot depended on: is there a working bf16 primitive to build an
attention block on. Yes.

--- 3. A failed detour, and why it doesn't change finding 2 ---

The Makefile-driven `make ... run` path for `basic/matrix_multiplication/single_core`
builds a separate C++ host test binary (test.cpp, linked against the XRT C++ SDK) and
runs the design through it instead of the pure-Python path. That path produced
byte-identical garbage "Reference" tables (bit patterns in the bf16 NaN/Inf exponent
range) and ~239k/262k element mismatches for BOTH bf16 (512x512x512) and the Makefile's
own i16/i32 default (512x512x512) -- same garbage regardless of dtype. Real NPU timing
still came through this path before verification ran (2511us bf16 / 3022us i16), but
the identical-garbage-regardless-of-dtype signature means this is a bug in the C++ test
harness or its interaction with this exact Windows XRT SDK build, not a bf16-specific
or kernel-level bug -- confirmed by getting a clean PASS through the alternate,
already-validated pure-Python path (`python3 <design>.py ...`, no Makefile, no
test.exe) that every other design in this repo's mlir-aie history has used
successfully. Not investigated further; use the pure-Python path for any future design
in this family, not `make run`.

--- 4. Five new Windows-native toolchain quirks fixed this session (invocation-time,
no upstream edits except one line noted below) ---

1. GNU Make auto-exports command-line-set variables into every recipe subprocess's
   environment. `basic/matrix_multiplication`'s Makefiles take both uppercase GEMM
   dims (M/K/N) and lowercase tile dims (m/k/n) on the command line; passing both
   together makes MSBuild's .NET `ProcessStartInfo.EnvironmentVariables` (a
   case-insensitive Hashtable on Windows) throw "Key in dictionary: 'N' Key being
   added: 'n'" while building the C++ host test.exe. Fix: don't pass the lowercase
   tile dims on the command line -- they already default to 32/32/32 in every
   Makefile checked, so omitting them changes nothing but avoids the collision. This
   only bites the C++ CMake host-build path (single_core/whole_array/cascade); it
   doesn't affect the pure-Python path at all.
2. `programming_examples/makefile-common`'s `command -v powershell.exe` WSL-detection
   heuristic false-positives on native Windows (same class of bug as the known
   `getwslpath` issue) and sets `powershell = powershell.exe`, wrapping the
   `cmake -E env CXXFLAGS="..."` build step through `powershell.exe`. PowerShell's
   own re-tokenization of that wrapped command line loses the CXXFLAGS quote
   boundary, so cmake -E env sees `-ggdb` etc. as separate unknown options instead of
   part of the CXXFLAGS value. Fix at invocation time: `make ... powershell= ...` (in
   addition to the already-known `getwslpath=echo`) to run the whole recipe directly
   through Git Bash instead.
3. The matmul family's CMakeLists.txt needs `-DXRT_INC_DIR=... -DXRT_LIB_DIR=...`, but
   the Makefile recipe never forwards Make command-line variables of those names into
   the actual `cmake` configure invocation -- setting them via `make XRT_INC_DIR=...`
   is a silent no-op. `common.cmake` does auto-detect via `find_package(XRT QUIET)`
   first, though, and that succeeds once `CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH` (a real environment
   variable, inherited by the child `cmake` process regardless of Make's variable
   semantics) points at the XRT SDK root: `export
   CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH=/c/Xilinx/XRT/xrt_sdk/xrt` -> `-- Found XRT: .../xrt_sdk/xrt/
   share/cmake/XRT (found version "2.21.0")`.
4. `xclbinutil.exe` (needed by aiecc's Python compile pipeline, independent of the
   above) lives at `C:\Xilinx\XRT\xrt_sdk\xrt\xclbinutil.exe`, not anywhere under
   `ironenv`. Put that directory on `PATH`.
5. `pyxrt.pyd` (the Python XRT binding used by every pure-Python IRON design's `--dev
   npu` path) lives at `C:\Xilinx\XRT\xrt_sdk\xrt\python\pyxrt.pyd` and is not
   importable by default -- `import pyxrt` fails with a plain `ModuleNotFoundError`,
   and the tensor factory in `aie.utils.tensor_factory` silently downgrades to a
   CPU-only tensor class on that failure (`ValueError: Unsupported device: npu`, not
   an import error, is what actually surfaces). Fix: put that directory on
   `PYTHONPATH` before running any `--dev npu` design.

One upstream source edit, in the external clone only (not part of this repo, not
committed here, will be lost on any `git pull` of mlir-aie -- re-apply if needed):
`programming_examples/basic/matrix_multiplication/common.h:382` had
`return (struct error<Tout>){row, col, expected, actual};` -- a GCC-only compound
literal over a class type, not standard C++. MSVC rejects it (C4576/C2760). Changed to
`return error<Tout>{row, col, expected, actual};` (plain aggregate brace-init,
semantically identical, portable). This was needed to get the C++ test.exe to compile
at all, before section 3 above found that path numerically broken regardless.

--- What is still not built ---

No fused attention kernel exists yet. This session validated that the primitives it
would be built from are real and correct on this hardware, got one real throughput
anchor (895 GFLOPS bf16, 4-column whole_array matmul, 512x512x512), and cleared five
Windows-toolchain blockers that would otherwise cost the next session the same hours.
The actual design -- QK^T, softmax, PV chained via layers_conv2_x's ObjectFifo pattern,
one xclbin, one dispatch, no LayerNorm (aie2 has none) -- has not been written. The
baseline this needs to beat also has not been decided: the net must be ONNX-exportable
and Quark-quantizable so IRON / VitisAI-EP-xint8 / CPU can be compared on the same
graph, same input.
